\section{Absence Is a Result, Not a Starting Point}

An unreachable instruction can be a useful diagnostic or a candidate for removing a block. But the test is not ``no incoming edge in the input.'' A block can have a predecessor in an unreachable cycle, and the entry is reachable without any predecessor. First compute reachability in the control-flow graph (CFG), then take its complement relative to the blocks actually present in a function. This is a small example of separating a fixed-point computation from its consumer. Khedker, Sanyal, and Karkare's dataflow text makes the choice of flow direction and merge operation explicit; here the propagation is forward and existential.

For positive Datalog over a finite set of constants, adding facts to a body relation cannot invalidate an existing ground rule instance. With input \(E\) fixed, the inflationary operator \(F_E(I)=I\cup T_E(I)\), where \(T_E\) produces one round of rule consequences, is monotone in the current derived facts \(I\) under set inclusion. Iteration from the empty set stabilizes at the least fixed point. Adding an input edge also preserves positive reachability conclusions. Neither statement implies that a complement or an exact count is preserved by an input addition \cite{abiteboul1995foundations}.

\section{Strata for a Control-Flow Report}

This Souffl\'e program models two functions, assigning globally distinct IDs to their blocks. The extractor guarantees that edges have declared endpoints in the same function and that entries belong to their functions. The extra \texttt{block} join makes the function boundary explicit in the recursive rule.

\begin{verbatim}
.decl fn(f:symbol)
.decl block(f:symbol, b:symbol)
.decl entry(f:symbol, b:symbol)
.decl edge(a:symbol, b:symbol)
.decl reach(f:symbol, b:symbol)
.decl unreachable(f:symbol, b:symbol)
.decl dead_count(f:symbol, n:number)
.output unreachable
.output dead_count

fn("main"). fn("leaf").
block("main","m0"). block("main","m1").
block("main","m2"). block("main","m3").
block("leaf","l0").
entry("main","m0"). entry("leaf","l0").
edge("m0","m1"). edge("m2","m3"). edge("m3","m2").

reach(F,B) :- entry(F,B).
reach(F,B) :- reach(F,A), edge(A,B), block(F,B).
unreachable(F,B) :- block(F,B), !reach(F,B).
dead_count(F,N) :- fn(F), N = count : { unreachable(F,B) }.
\end{verbatim}

The output facts are \verb|unreachable("main","m2")| and \verb|unreachable("main","m3")|, and the counts are \verb|dead_count("main",2)| and \verb|dead_count("leaf",0)|. The positive binder \verb|block(F,B)| limits negation to valid block pairs. The positive \verb|fn(F)| also matters: it enumerates functions even if there are no unreachable rows for one of them. The local aggregate variable \texttt{B} counts one row per distinct unreachable block, not the number of ways to reach or fail to reach it. Count on an empty group gives zero; for summaries such as minimum, specify how the absence of any contributing row is represented rather than assuming a numeric default.

\section{Order Dependencies Before Evaluating Rules}

Assign a stratum number to each predicate. A head must be at least as high as its positive body predicates and strictly higher than any predicate it negates or reads through an ordinary exact aggregate. A negative or aggregate edge inside a strongly connected component prevents such an assignment. For example, a rule that reaches a successor only if that same successor is currently classified as unreachable would introduce a cycle through negation. It is not a repair to pick a more favorable order for those rules. In contrast, positive recursion can stay within one stratum.

\Cref{fig:visual-345} shows the two completion barriers: the complement requires settled reachability, and the count requires a settled complement.

