\section{Recheck After an Edge Edit}

\begin{verbatim}
snapshot := freeze_validated_cfg()    # one version of all input facts
reached  := close_entries(snapshot)  # positive rounds to saturation
dead     := snapshot.blocks - reached
report   := count_per(snapshot.functions, dead)  # include zeros
publish(report, snapshot.version)   # never mix strata across edits
\end{verbatim}

Add \verb|edge("m1","m2").| and recompute. Both previously unreachable blocks become reachable; the unreachable relation is empty and \verb|dead_count("main",0)| replaces \verb|dead_count("main",2)|. Deleting that bridge restores the two unreachable facts. The cycle does not justify itself: every reachable block requires a finite proof from an entry. An incremental implementation that merely decrements incoming-edge counts can incorrectly keep both cycle members alive. Full recomputation is a suitable reference result for an optimizer or editor integration.

Even on growing groups, an exact count represented as a tuple replaces its old tuple rather than extending a set of tuples. A threshold such as ``at least two distinct witnesses'' can instead be encoded positively by joining two rows and testing their block IDs for inequality. That threshold is monotone \emph{in the witness relation}; it is not monotone in additions to the CFG if the witnesses are unreachable blocks. Lattice-based aggregation can sometimes use a different order and a monotone update, but that is a separate semantics, not a license to put ordinary \texttt{count} in a recursive cycle. Finally, removal of a block is a transformation of the input IR, not the deletion of a derived diagnostic; a real CFG must model exceptional edges, indirect targets, and all externally reachable entries before absence is actionable.

Accordingly, an optimizer should consume a settled unreachable report as a certificate for a particular IR version, then re-extract and reanalyze after rewriting rather than treating the old complement as a persistent fact.
